// Reading a `.pack` — and a workaround whose cause was removed by the same file that kept it.
//
// `packages/git/src/pack.wac` is 662 lines and unusually careful: it explains that a delta's header
// size is *"the size of the data at this offset, which for a delta is the delta and not the object"*
// and checks both numbers against two different `git` commands, because *"a reader that confused them
// would agree with one oracle and not the other."* None of that is rewritten.
//
// What is rewritten is a field that should not exist any more, and the reason it survives is worth
// more than the field.
//
// ## Two paths need one fact and answer it two ways
//
// Neither the pack format nor an object's header says how long its compressed bytes are. The file's
// own header states the consequence:
//
//   > Nothing in the object header says how long the compressed bytes are, and `packages/gzip`'s
//   > `inflate` does not report what it consumed. So an object's stream is bounded by **the next
//   > object's offset**, taken from the index — which is why `openPack` sorts the offsets and keeps
//   > them.
//
// Three hundred lines later, in the section that indexes a pack arriving without one:
//
//   > because nothing records where each object's compressed bytes end, ask the inflater.
//   > `packages/gzip`'s `inflateAt` reports that, which is **the export this needed and the reason it
//   > exists**.
//
// So `inflateAt` was added for the path that has no index to consult, and the path that *has* one was
// never migrated. Both sentences are in one file and the first is no longer true.
//
// ## What the un-migrated path pays
//
// `Pack` carries `i64[] byOffset`, *"every object's offset, ascending — what bounds each compressed
// stream"*, and a binary search over it to find the next offset above a given one. Then, to read one
// object:
//
//     u8[] slice = u8[upto - from]();
//     for (i32 i = 0; i < slice.len(); i++) { slice[i] = p.bytes[from + i]; }
//     u8[] data = inflate(slice);
//
// **Every compressed object is copied into a fresh array before being inflated**, because `inflate`
// takes a whole buffer and the bound has to be applied by slicing. `inflateAt(p.bytes, from)` needs
// neither the copy, nor the array, nor the search, nor the field — and it is already imported at the
// top of this file.
//
// That copy loop is also one of the 750 in `issues/system/0352a`, and this is the version of that
// issue with the most to say: it is not a loop that should have been `copyFrom`, it is a loop that
// should not exist.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * A pack and its index.
 *
 * **`byOffset` is gone**, along with the binary search over it. It existed to bound a compressed
 * stream, `inflateAt` bounds it, and nothing else read the field. This is the only deletion in this
 * directory that needs no language change and no design argument — just the migration the shipped
 * file started and did not finish.
 */
export struct Pack {
  Bytes bytes;
  PackIndex idx;
}

/**
 * What an object header says it is.
 *
 * Three bits on the wire — *"1 commit, 2 tree, 3 blob, 4 tag, 6 offset-delta, 7 reference-delta. 5 is
 * unused and 0 is invalid."* A closed set with a **hole at 5** and an **invalid 0**, which is the
 * sixth closed-set-spelled-openly here and the first with either feature. See below: it sharpens the
 * `tls` ask rather than repeating it.
 */
export enum PackedKind {
  Commit,
  Tree,
  Blob,
  Tag,
  OffsetDelta(i64 back),
  ReferenceDelta(Bytes name),
}

/**
 * A size read from an object header.
 *
 * **Not an `Offset`, and the shipped file keeps them apart by hand.** Its header:
 *
 * > **Two varints that are not the same varint.** The size above is little-endian groups:
 * > `size |= (b & 0x7f) << shift`. An offset delta encodes its distance differently —
 * > `ofs = ((ofs + 1) << 7) | (b & 0x7f)` — which is not the same function and has no zero-length
 * > representation, so reusing one decoder for both silently misreads any chain longer than 127
 * > bytes. They are two functions here for that reason.
 *
 * Two decoders, two return types now, and the confusion the comment guards against stops being
 * expressible.
 */
export struct Size { i64 of; }

/** A distance backwards from the current object, as an offset delta encodes it. */
export struct Backref { i64 of; }

export struct PackIndex { Bytes bytes; }

/** Why a pack cannot be read. */
export union<Damaged, Truncated, UnknownKind, ThinPack> PackFault;

export struct Damaged { string why; i64 at; }
export struct Truncated { i64 at; i64 wanted; }
export struct UnknownKind { i32 saw; i64 at; }

/**
 * One or more reference deltas name a base the pack does not contain.
 *
 * Kept as a member with a payload because the shipped comment is right that this is not damage:
 * *"A thin pack is not a damaged one, and telling them apart is the whole reason this variant
 * exists."*
 */
export struct ThinPack { Bytes[] wanted; }

export Result<Pack, PackFault> open(Bytes packBytes, Bytes idxBytes) { … }
export Result<Bytes, PackFault> object(const Pack p, i64 at) { … }
export Result<PackIndex, PackFault> index(Bytes packBytes) { … }

// ## What could not be written
//
// ### A closed set with a hole and an invalid zero
//
// `PackedKind` above has six members and the wire has eight codes: `0` invalid, `5` unused, and six
// real ones. `@/packages/tls`'s entry asked for **declared, stable discriminants** because a phase is
// serialised; this needs that *and* two more things it did not:
//
//   - **a gap.** `OffsetDelta = 6` after `Tag = 4` is not a reordering hazard, it is a hole the format
//     specifies and a future version may fill. An enum numbered by declaration order cannot express
//     it; one with declared numbers can, and then `5` has no name, which is correct.
//   - **an invalid value that is not a variant.** `0` is not *unknown type five*, it is *this is not
//     an object header*. So the checked conversion `tls` asked for is doing two jobs here — rejecting
//     a code with no meaning, and rejecting a code reserved for later — and a caller repairing a pack
//     might want to tell them apart.
//
// So the ask is unchanged in kind and larger in detail, and this file is the reason to write
// `enum K : u8 { A = 1, … }` rather than `enum K { A, … }` even when the numbers happen to be dense
// today.
//
// ### Two varints with one signature, and the type that would separate them costs an allocation
//
// `Size` and `Backref` above are one-field wrappers over `i64` whose only job is to stop the two
// decoders being confused — the confusion the shipped header spends a paragraph warning about. They
// are the same device as `@/packages/tor`'s two identity fields and `@/packages/fmt`'s two `FixedBig`
// capacities: **a distinct type per meaning, where the representation is identical.**
//
// And the cost is real and unpriced. A wrapper struct is a heap object, so a pack with 100,000 objects
// allocates 100,000 `Size` values to read their headers, against zero for a bare `i64`. Every previous
// entry that reached for this device reached for it on a value that is already a reference — a
// `Bytes`, a `Vec` — where the wrapper is free. **This is the first place the newtype is over a
// primitive in a hot loop**, and this directory has no measurement for it.
//
// `../../../bench/nullablecost.wac` measured the nearest thing — a boxed `i32?` at 1.4 ns per non-null
// value — and a one-field struct should be the same allocation. At 100,000 objects that is about
// 0.14 ms per pack walk, which is probably nothing against inflating them; *probably* is doing work
// there and a bench would settle it. Recorded as unpriced rather than argued either way.
//
// ### And the deletion above is the finding, not the types
//
// The three entries above are language questions. The `byOffset` removal is not: it needs nothing,
// breaks nothing, and removes a field, a binary search, an allocation and a copy per object read.
//
// It survived because **the sentence justifying it is in the file header and the sentence retiring it
// is three hundred lines down**, and nothing reads a file looking for the second contradicting the
// first. That is the same failure as `packages/wacc/src/kinds.wac`'s stale constraint and
// `packages/fmt/src/bigint.wac`'s stale cross-reference, and the third instance today where the
// correction was *already present in the tree* and had not reached the thing it corrected.
//
// Checked before claiming it: `endOf` has exactly one caller, at `:193`, and `byOffset` is read
// nowhere else. `packages/git/src/receive.wac` also has an `endOf` — a different function with a
// different signature over a pkt-line payload — which is two unrelated meanings under one name in one
// package, the shape `issues/system/0348a` counted in `tools/`. Not the finding here, and worth the
// sentence because it is what made the check necessary.
//
// Filed as `issues/system/0357a`.
